## Claim
Text: "Twitter now putting warnings on tweets that are just unedited clips of Biden saying he would end fracking"
Claim author: Zach Parkinson
Claim date: October 23, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did Twitter have an active policy in October 2020 to add warning labels or notices to certain tweets containing political content?
Answer: Yes. Twitter updated its Civic Integrity Policy on October 9, 2020, with the labeling policy going into effect on October 16, 2020. As the source states, "Tweets which include premature claims of a victory in an election will be labeled and direct people to our official US Election page in Explore," and labels such as "this claim is disputed" would "primarily target tweets that include false or misleading information about how to participate in an election, 'information intended to undermine public confidence in an election,' and information intended to intimidate or dissuade people from participating in an election or other civic process." This policy explicitly applied to presidential candidates' tweets as well.

Source URL: https://www.popsugar.com/news/twitter-integrity-policy-election-retweets-update-47868604/1000

### Did Twitter's policy at that time apply specifically to unedited video clips, or was it limited to manipulated or misleading media?
Answer: According to the source, Twitter's policy unveiled in February 2020 specifically targeted "synthetic and manipulated media" that is "deceptively share[d]" and likely to cause harm. The policy was designed to address deepfakes and "cheapfakes" (low-tech edits meant to deceive), and Twitter's VP of trust and safety stated that the policy "is really focused on the media itself. So if the media is altered or fabricated...the policy would still apply." There is no indication in this source that the policy applied to unedited clips of real statements—instead, the focus was explicitly on content that was altered, manipulated, or fabricated.

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/02/04/twitter-unveils-new-rules-to-tackle-deepfakes-ahead-of-2020-election.html

### Did Joe Biden make statements on the campaign trail about fracking that could be shown in unedited video clips?
Answer: Yes, Joe Biden made statements on the campaign trail about fracking that could be shown in unedited video clips. Most notably, during a March Democratic presidential candidate debate, Biden stated "No more—no new fracking" when restating his position after initially seeming to agree with Bernie Sanders's call for an end to all U.S. fracking. Additionally, Biden's published energy platform stated that no new fracking projects would be permitted "on public lands and waters."

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/video-resurfaces-kamala-harris-saying-she-wants-ban-fracking-after-saying-biden-will-not-during-1537304

### What was the specific type of warning or label that Twitter was applying to tweets in October 2020, and what were the criteria for its application?
Answer: According to the search result, in 2020 Twitter began applying labels (along with possible removal) to tweets containing synthetic or manipulated media, using a "three-pronged test" to determine how the content should be treated: whether the content is synthetic or manipulated, whether it is shared in a deceptive manner, and whether it is likely to cause serious harm. Content meeting fewer criteria would be labeled as altered or fabricated, may direct users to additional context, or show a warning before users retweet or like the post, as described: "Content that meets fewer criteria is more likely to be labeled as altered or fabricated. It also may direct users to additional context or show a warning to users before they retweet or like the post."

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/02/04/twitter-unveils-new-rules-to-tackle-deepfakes-ahead-of-2020-election.html

### Did Twitter add warning labels specifically to tweets containing unedited video clips of Joe Biden discussing fracking around October 23, 2020?
Answer: No. The search result indicates that around that time, Twitter added warning labels to a **deceptively edited** video of Biden—not an unedited clip about fracking. The flagged video, tweeted by White House social media director Dan Scavino, was cut to make it appear Biden said "we can only re-elect Donald Trump" when his actual full statement was "we can only re-elect Donald Trump if, in fact, we get engaged in this circular firing squad here. It's got to be a positive campaign." As the article explains, the clip was "a 'cheap fake,' a piece of content that's been sped up, slowed down, cut off or easily edited in a way that deceptively strips it of content or changes the meaning," and Twitter's site integrity team determined it "broke Twitter's manipulated content rules." The claim's characterization of the labeled tweets as "unedited clips of Biden saying he would end fracking" does not match the documented incident.

Source URL: https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/social-media/facebook-twitter-put-warning-label-edited-video-biden-n1153506

### Did Twitter issue any official public statements or press releases about adding warnings to tweets with political video content in October 2020?
Answer: Yes. According to the NPR article from October 9, 2020, Twitter officials Vijaya Gadde and Kayvon Beykpour published an official blog post announcing the expansion of warning labels to slow the spread of election misinformation, including hiding tweets with false claims from American political figures behind warning screens. However, the article focuses on election misinformation generally rather than specifically addressing manipulated video content like the Biden fracking clip referenced in the claim.

Source URL: https://www.npr.org/2020/10/09/922028482/twitter-expands-warning-labels-to-slow-spread-of-election-misinformation

### Was Twitter's warning label policy part of its announced efforts to combat misinformation ahead of the November 2020 US presidential election?
Answer: Yes, Twitter's warning label policy was part of its announced efforts to combat misinformation ahead of the November 2020 US presidential election. According to the source, "Ahead of the 2020 presidential election, Twitter has announced an update to its Civic Integrity Policy to prevent the spread of misinformation as it pertains to voting and election results." The new labeling policy, which went into effect on October 16, 2020, targeted tweets containing false or misleading information about election participation, content intended to undermine public confidence in the election, and information meant to intimidate or dissuade people from participating in civic processes.

Source URL: https://www.popsugar.com/news/twitter-integrity-policy-election-retweets-update-47868604/1000

### How did Twitter distinguish between unedited clips and manipulated media when deciding to apply warning labels in October 2020?
Answer: According to the source, Twitter's policy focused on whether content was "synthetic or manipulated" — that is, altered or fabricated — rather than simply unedited clips. The company applied a "three-pronged test" to determine if media violated its policy, including whether it was likely to cause serious harm, whether it was shared deceptively, and whether it was synthetic or manipulated. Content that was genuinely unedited would not meet the definition of synthetic or manipulated media, so it would fall outside the policy's scope, while altered or fabricated content (such as slowed-down or doctored videos) could be labeled, given a warning, or removed depending on how many criteria were met.

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/02/04/twitter-unveils-new-rules-to-tackle-deepfakes-ahead-of-2020-election.html

## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence clearly shows that Twitter's policy in October 2020 specifically targeted **manipulated or deceptively edited media** (synthetic content, "cheapfakes," deepfakes), not unedited clips of real statements. The policy used a "three-pronged test" focused on whether content was synthetic/manipulated, shared deceptively, and likely to cause harm. The specific incident around October 23, 2020, where Twitter applied a warning label to a Biden-related video, involved a **deceptively edited clip** (cut to make it appear Biden said "we can only re-elect Donald Trump" when his actual full statement was different) — not an unedited clip about fracking. While Biden did make real statements about fracking on the campaign trail, there is no evidence that Twitter labeled any unedited clips of those statements.

## Missing Information

No significant information is lacking. The evidence directly addresses both the nature of Twitter's labeling policy and the specific incident that likely prompted the claim.

## Conclusion

The claim is refuted on two grounds: (1) Twitter's policy explicitly targeted manipulated/edited media, not unedited clips, and (2) the actual labeled video was a deceptively edited clip about a different topic (not fracking). The claim mischaracterizes both the nature of the labeled content and the subject matter of the tweet.

