### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted because Twitter's October 2020 labeling policy explicitly targeted **manipulated or deceptively edited media**—not unedited clips of real statements—using a "three-pronged test" focused on whether content was synthetic/manipulated, shared deceptively, and likely to cause harm ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2020/02/04/twitter-unveils-new-rules-to-tackle-deepfakes-ahead-of-2020-election.html)). The specific incident around October 23, 2020, involved Twitter labeling a **deceptively edited "cheapfake"** video of Biden—cut to make it appear he said "we can only re-elect Donald Trump" when his actual full statement was different—not an unedited clip about fracking ([NBC News](https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/social-media/facebook-twitter-put-warning-label-edited-video-biden-n1153506)). While Biden did make real statements about fracking on the campaign trail ([Newsweek](https://www.newsweek.com/video-resurfaces-kamala-harris-saying-she-wants-ban-fracking-after-saying-biden-will-not-during-1537304)), there is no evidence Twitter labeled any unedited clips of those statements, and Twitter's broader Civic Integrity Policy update targeted election misinformation rather than unedited political speech ([PopSugar](https://www.popsugar.com/news/twitter-integrity-policy-election-retweets-update-47868604/1000), [NPR](https://www.npr.org/2020/10/09/922028482/twitter-expands-warning-labels-to-slow-spread-of-election-misinformation)). The claim mischaracterizes both the nature of the labeled content (edited, not unedited) and the subject matter (not fracking).